  "editorial_note": "GitHub listed fifteen projects this Sunday morning. Nine of them were featured here before, most within the past week, so today's picks come from the other six: a ==local, cloud-free Home Assistant bridge for Samsung appliances==, a context-compression layer for long coding-agent sessions, Meta's newly public design system, NVIDIA's preview Kubernetes engine for GPU fleets, the long-running Sherlock username search and Tailwind CSS after two point releases. Curation order reflects practical interest, not GitHub rank. Trending is not a release date or an endorsement.",
  "coverage_notes": [
    "All 15 listing entries were inspected. Exact public GitHub API metadata was checked at about 07:01 UTC for the six entries never featured here and for two earlier picks (cloudflare/security-audit-skill and milind-soni/OpenMausBot), along with their recent releases; push and release dates of four other earlier picks were read at about 07:03 UTC; README files were read for the six picks. Every checked repository was active and unarchived. GitHub's API detects MIT or Apache-2.0 for five picks; for ranxianglei/billion-context it reports NOASSERTION, and the LICENSE file was read: it is the MIT text plus an added attribution term for user-facing products.",
    "Nine entries featured before were left out. Five were picked in the past seven days and have published no release since: prometheus/prometheus, astral-sh/ruff and zed-industries/zed (7 November), vercel-labs/agent-browser (6 November; latest release v0.41.2 on 3 November) and google/skills (6 November; no releases). Four older picks were also left out: mattpocock/skills (31 October; latest release v1.3.2 on 29 October), anthropics/skills (31 October; no releases), cloudflare/security-audit-skill (21 October; last push 14 September) and milind-soni/OpenMausBot (30 October), which has shipped feature releases since, including a meeting-notes bot in v0.1.147 on 7 November; we preferred projects not yet covered and may revisit it.",
    "GitHub gives no reason for an entry's place on the list, and we do not guess why mature projects such as Sherlock and Tailwind CSS appear today.",
    "The preceding seven calendar dates, 1-7 November, were checked for local history; all seven editions exist.",
    "The official listing was captured at 08:01:06 Europe/Paris. Literal stars-today labels are GitHub's displayed period figures; API total stars and README evidence were checked separately at the times shown per card. They are not an independently audited rolling 24-hour growth measurement.",
    "Adoption, compression and benchmark figures are the maintainers' own, including Astryx's 13,000+ internal apps and billion-context's token-savings and benchmark results published in its own repository. No candidate code, installer, binary, plugin or model was installed, downloaded or run, and none of this is a security audit or endorsement."
  ],
